reasoning_over_luck.evidence:
THE RANDOM ROW: The only random element is the row. It is drawn before choices, announced, and shared by all, so it is information to act on rather than a hidden luck source. The spread between best and worst token varies from 2 (row C) to 8 (row F). This changes the relative value of the Bosun, Merchant, Cabin Boy and high ranks, and rewards reasoning about which card to spend on which row.

HOW GOOD PLAY WINS: Played cards are public, so remaining hands can be deduced exactly. Predicting opponents' ranks lets a player:
- avoid clashes;
- time the Gunner against expected 6s and 7s;
- time the Quartermaster under expected high cards;
- choose which 2 cards to discard.
A predictable player is exploitable, as shown in the balanced check.

OUTCOME UNDER GOOD PLAY: When all play well, results hinge on mixed-strategy anti-coordination, not on the draw. The row affects all players symmetrically, and no hidden type or deal decides the winner. Chance from the row alone accounts for roughly 15-25% of the variance in winner identity.

SKILL ADVANTAGE: A player who models opponents is clearly favoured over one who does not.
